# tools/e/encoding_bech32_witness.rish -- Bech32 (BIP-173) and Bech32m (BIP-350) authored in pure Rye: the checksummed binary-to-text form a
# modern address wears -- a human-readable prefix, a '1' separator, the payload in the case-safe 32-symbol alphabet, and a six-symbol BCH
# checksum -- proven against the BIP valid checksums, the canonical segwit example, and an independent table-driven polymod (encoding - the
# serialization sibling of Season G - Cryptography).
#
# Season: encoding -- the text-form sibling of the crypto library, beside base64.rye, base58.rye, base58check.rye, base32.rye, and hex.rye.
# Where crypto/ authors the mathematics that produces bytes, encoding/ authors the text those bytes travel in, keeping the same parity
# discipline: authored from the standard, calling no std codec in the primitive, proven byte-for-byte against a reference runnable now.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md (the wave that seats the Rye-first spine).
# Clean-room: the alphabet, generator, and checksum law are BIP-173 and BIP-350's own; the arithmetic is ours (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# encoding/bech32.rye authors Bech32 and Bech32m from BIP-173 and BIP-350 -- the "qpzry9x8gf2tvdw0s3jn54khce6mua7l" alphabet, the BCH polymod
# over GF(32) with its five-term generator, the two spec constants (1 for Bech32, 0x2bc830a3 for Bech32m) that version-bind a v0 segwit output
# apart from a BIP-350 payload, the hrp expansion, and convertbits regrouping. The primitive calls no std bech32 (Zig ships none). Parity is
# proven at selftest against three references runnable now: the BIP valid checksums (round-tripped to themselves lowercased and cross-refused
# across specs), the canonical segwit example bc1qw508d6qejxtdg4y5r3zarvary0c5xw7kv8f3t4 decoding to witness v0 and the 20-byte program
# 751e76e8199196d454941c45d1b3a323f1433bd6, and a second, INDEPENDENT table-driven polymod matching the per-bit residue byte-for-byte. Raw
# bytes round-trip through encode_bytes / decode_bytes. A flipped checksum refuses BadChecksum, mixed case MixedCase, a missing separator
# BadLength, an out-of-alphabet symbol BadChar, an empty hrp HrpEmpty, a too-small destination DestTooSmall. Purely local -- no key, no network,
# no funds, no real device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/e/encoding_bech32_witness.rish

say "encoding-bech32: Language EN — Bech32 (BIP-173) and Bech32m (BIP-350) are the checksummed binary-to-text form a modern address wears: a human-readable prefix (bc for Bitcoin, npub for a Nostr identity key, cosmos for a Cosmos account), a '1' separator, the payload in a case-safe 32-symbol alphabet, and a six-symbol BCH checksum that catches a single mistyped character where Base58Check catches it with a truncated double-SHA-256. The crypto library proves the key bytes; this module dresses them in the checksummed alphabet and back, byte-for-byte, so a proven Ed25519 key becomes an npub the moment a surface asks."
say "encoding-bech32: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively: the polymod folds the expanded hrp, the data, and six trailing zeros through a five-term generator over GF(32) and XORs a spec constant (1 for Bech32, 0x2bc830a3 for Bech32m); decoding recomputes the residue and demands the constant back, so the same bytes always render to the same address and the same address always recovers the same bytes — and a v0 segwit output is refused under Bech32m exactly as BIP-350 binds it."
say "encoding-bech32: Lens TAME — the primitive calls no std bech32 (Zig ships none); it is authored from the standard, bounded input (max_hrp_len, max_data_len, max_string_len, max_payload_bytes), contract asserts on every function, buffer-length checked before the first write. Parity holds against the BIP valid checksums AND the canonical segwit known-answer AND a second, independent table-driven polymod byte-for-byte over every vector; a flipped checksum refuses BadChecksum, mixed case MixedCase, a missing separator BadLength, an out-of-alphabet symbol BadChar, an empty hrp HrpEmpty, a too-small destination DestTooSmall — each before a partial result escapes. Purely local — no key, no network, no funds, no device."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "encoding/bin/bech32"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p encoding/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build encoding/bech32.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "encoding-bech32: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "encoding/bin/bech32.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "encoding-bech32: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "encoding-bech32: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN encoding-bech32' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "encoding-bech32: selftest did not print the GREEN encoding-bech32 line"
